{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_018v6XjWnYbYhV9z74y13Cc4","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_012ZnjAgqYU8U6zVNy6ESCYV","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R1: Methods + Experiments review","prompt":"You are Reviewer R1 for a top ML venue review. Your focus areas are **Methods** and **Experiments**. Review the paper below rigorously and independently. Score on a 6-point scale (1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept). Target acceptance rate ~25%.\n\n## Paper Summary\n\n**Title:** \"Stagnation Signals Are Structural: An Empirical Study of Progress Detection in LLM Agents via Weak Supervision\"\n\nThe paper studies stagnation detection in LLM coding agents (SWE-Agent, OpenHands) as a window-level binary classification problem (k=5 actions). It uses a weak supervision pipeline with 3 labeling functions (LFs): command repetition (CR), file diff absence (FD), outcome backpropagation (OB), aggregated by majority vote. Three detection methods are compared: 30 hand-engineered structural features (GB/RF/LR), neural embedding probes (GRU on Qwen3-0.6B/E5/BGE-m3), and LLM-as-judge (Claude Sonnet zero-shot).\n\n**Key Claims:**\n1. Structural features dominate: in-domain macro-F1 0.995, cross-agent 0.967, outperforming probes (0.906/0.751) and LLM-judge (0.663) by large margins.\n2. LF temporal dynamics are systematic: CR leads onset by ~1.87 steps, OB lags by +6.7 steps on SWE-Agent, but roles reverse on OpenHands.\n3. Temporal label models (time-weighted voting, HMM, lag-aware HMM) fail to improve over MV despite well-characterized i.i.d. violations — a well-motivated negative result.\n\n**Gold Set Validation (N=650):**\n- 2 annotators, κ_inter=0.82, agreement 91%\n- Gold-LF agreement: κ=0.85, 92% agreement, monotonically increasing by confidence (83%→96%→98%)\n- On gold: Structural GB 0.91, Probe BGE 0.85, LLM-judge 0.68 — hierarchy preserved\n- Circularity gap ~8.5pp for structural, ~8.6-9.6pp for probes, LLM-judge +1.8pp (orthogonal errors)\n- Feature importance ranking stable (Spearman ρ=0.96 between LF-derived and gold evaluations)\n\n**Additional Experiments in Paper:**\n- Cross-agent structural validation via bounded circularity transfer (OH gold est. 0.87-0.92)\n- F1_stag per-class analysis: by confidence (0.863 at conf=2), trajectory position (U-shaped: 0.83/0.80/0.88), stagnation subtype (repetitive 0.91, no-file 0.84, outcome-only 0.73)\n- Error decomposition: structural 7 errors vs probe 131 errors on LF-derived; strict subset relationship\n- Temporal onset alignment on gold: 82% overall, CR leading (-1.5 steps), FD concurrent (-0.8), OB lagging (+7.4)\n\n**Experiment Coverage vs. Imagined Experiments:**\nThe review_context.yaml lists these imagined experiments (all present in the paper):\n1. Gold-LF Agreement (Cohen's κ) — ✓ covered (Tables 1-2)\n2. Gold-LF Conf≥2 Accuracy — ✓ covered (Table 3)\n3. Structural Features on Gold Labels — ✓ covered (Tables 4-5)\n4. Probe on Gold Labels — ✓ covered (Table 6)\n5. LLM-Judge on Gold Labels — ✓ covered (Table 6)\n6. Gold Temporal Onset Alignment — ✓ covered (Table 8)\n7. Gold Cross-Agent Structural Validation — ✓ covered (Table 9)\n8. Gold-Validated Circularity Quantification — ✓ covered (Table 7)\n9. F1_stag Per-Class Breakdown by Confidence — ✓ covered (Table 10)\n10. F1_stag by Trajectory Position — ✓ covered (Table 11)\n11. F1_stag by Stagnation Subtype — ✓ covered (Table 12)\n12. F1_stag Probe vs Structural Error Decomposition — ✓ covered (Table 13)\n\nAll 12 imagined experiments appear to be covered.\n\n## Your Review Task (R1: Methods + Experiments)\n\nPlease evaluate:\n1. **Methodological soundness**: Is the weak supervision pipeline well-designed? Are the 3 LFs reasonable and complementary? Is the MV aggregation appropriate?\n2. **Experimental rigor**: Are the comparisons fair? Are there confounds? Is the statistical analysis adequate (e.g., confidence intervals, significance tests)?\n3. **Circularity concern**: The paper acknowledges that structural features overlap with LF inputs. Does the gold set validation adequately address this? Is 650 windows sufficient?\n4. **Cross-agent generalization**: The OH gold estimate is bounded (0.87-0.92) rather than directly measured. Is this methodology sound?\n5. **Negative result (temporal models)**: Is the analysis of why temporal models fail convincing?\n6. **Completeness**: Are important baselines or analyses missing?\n7. **Reproducibility**: Is there enough detail to reproduce?\n\nOutput your review in this format:\n\n### R1 Review: Methods + Experiments\n\n**Summary** (2-3 sentences)\n\n**Strengths** (numbered list)\n\n**Weaknesses** (numbered list)\n\n**Questions for Authors** (numbered list)\n\n**Minor Issues** (numbered list)\n\n**Soundness Score** (1-4 scale: 1=poor, 2=fair, 3=good, 4=excellent)\n**Overall Score** (1-6 scale)\n**Confidence** (1-5 scale: 1=low, 5=expert)\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":30137,"cache_read_input_tokens":22909,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":30137},"output_tokens":16,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4375801c-8206-4a72-bdc9-833c067fbe9b","uuid":"e48d89ec-99d7-4a53-a772-251de2e549bd"}
