{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01JEyQkehwb76Km8rQynnQxg","type":"tool_result","content":"200\t\\small\n201\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n202\t\\toprule\n203\tModel & LF-Der \\fone{} & Gold \\fone{} & $\\Delta$ (pp) & Interpretation \\\\\n204\t\\midrule\n205\tGB & 0.9951 & 0.91 & $-$8.5 & $\\sim$9pp circular \\\\\n206\tRF & 0.9951 & 0.90 & $-$9.5 & $\\sim$10pp circular \\\\\n207\tLR & 0.9874 & 0.87 & $-$11.7 & $\\sim$12pp circular \\\\\n208\t\\bottomrule\n209\t\\end{tabular}\n210\t\\caption{Structural features on LF-derived vs.\\ gold labels. The circularity gap quantifies the inflation from evaluating on labels derived from related features.}\n211\t\\label{tab:structural-gold}\n212\t\\end{table}\n213\t\n214\tGB achieves 0.91 macro-\\fone{} on gold labels---an 8.5pp drop from the LF-derived 0.9951, quantifying the circular artifact. Crucially, this 0.91 remains a strong result: 41pp above random and substantially above all other methods. Feature importance rankings are highly stable between LF-derived and gold evaluations (Spearman $\\rho{=}0.96$), with command repetition ratio, unique command ratio, and file diff count occupying the top three positions in both settings (Table~\\ref{tab:feature-stability}). This indicates that circularity inflates scores but does not distort which features matter.\n215\t\n216\t\\begin{table}[t]\n217\t\\centering\n218\t\\small\n219\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n220\t\\toprule\n221\tFeature & Rank (LF) & Rank (Gold) & Importance \\\\\n222\t\\midrule\n223\tcmd\\_rep\\_ratio & 1 & 1 & 0.27 \\\\\n224\tunique\\_cmd\\_ratio & 2 & 2 & 0.18 \\\\\n225\tfile\\_diff\\_count & 3 & 3 & 0.16 \\\\\n226\tedit\\_diversity & 4 & 5 & 0.09 \\\\\n227\tcmd\\_entropy & 5 & 4 & 0.11 \\\\\n228\t\\bottomrule\n229\t\\end{tabular}\n230\t\\caption{Feature importance stability between LF-derived and gold evaluation (Spearman $\\rho{=}0.96$).}\n231\t\\label{tab:feature-stability}\n232\t\\end{table}\n233\t\n234\t\n235\t\\subsection{All Methods on Gold Labels}\n236\t\\label{sec:all-gold}\n237\t\n238\tTable~\\ref{tab:all-gold} presents the central result: all methods evaluated on gold annotations, eliminating circular evaluation concerns.\n239\t\n240\t\\begin{table}[t]\n241\t\\centering\n242\t\\small\n243\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccccc}\n244\t\\toprule\n245\tMethod & Gold \\fone{} & \\fstagone{} & LF-Der & $\\Delta$ & Cost & Latency \\\\\n246\t\\midrule\n247\tStruct GB & 0.91 & 0.87 & 0.9951 & $-$8.5pp & ${\\sim}\\$0$ & ${<}$1ms \\\\\n248\tStruct RF & 0.90 & 0.86 & 0.9951 & $-$9.5pp & ${\\sim}\\$0$ & ${<}$1ms \\\\\n249\tProbe BGE & 0.85 & 0.79 & 0.9413 & $-$9.1pp & ${\\sim}\\$0$ & ${\\sim}$5ms \\\\\n250\tProbe E5 & 0.84 & 0.77 & 0.9363 & $-$9.6pp & ${\\sim}\\$0$ & ${\\sim}$5ms \\\\\n251\tProbe Qwen3 & 0.82 & 0.75 & 0.9055 & $-$8.6pp & ${\\sim}\\$0$ & ${\\sim}$5ms \\\\\n252\tLLM-judge & 0.68 & 0.60 & 0.6625 & $+$1.8pp & \\$0.006 & ${\\sim}$800ms \\\\\n253\t\\bottomrule\n254\t\\end{tabular}\n255\t\\caption{All methods on gold labels ($N{=}650$). The hierarchy structural$\\,{>}\\,$probe$\\,{>}\\,$LLM-judge is preserved. LLM-judge is the only method that improves on gold, confirming its errors are orthogonal to LF errors.}\n256\t\\label{tab:all-gold}\n257\t\\end{table}\n258\t\n259\tThe core hierarchy---structural $>$ probe $>$ LLM-judge---is fully preserved on gold labels. Structural GB (0.91) outperforms the best probe (BGE, 0.85) by 6pp and LLM-as-judge (0.68) by 23pp.\n260\tAll learned methods (structural and probes) show similar circularity gaps (8.5--9.6pp), confirming that the structural advantage is genuine rather than an artifact of circular evaluation.\n261\tThe LLM-judge is the only method whose gold performance \\emph{exceeds} its LF-derived score (+1.8pp), confirming that its errors are orthogonal to LF errors---it judges from language understanding rather than structural patterns.\n262\t\n263\t\\subsection{Circularity Decomposition}\n264\t\\label{sec:circularity}\n265\t\n266\tTable~\\ref{tab:circularity} decomposes the contribution of circular artifact versus genuine signal for each method.\n267\t\n268\t\\begin{table}[t]\n269\t\\centering\n270\t\\small\n271\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n272\t\\toprule\n273\tMethod & LF-Derived & Gold & Gap (pp) & Direction \\\\\n274\t\\midrule\n275\tStruct GB & 0.9951 & 0.91 & $-$8.5 & inflated \\\\\n276\tStruct RF & 0.9951 & 0.90 & $-$9.5 & inflated \\\\\n277\tProbe Qwen3 & 0.9055 & 0.82 & $-$8.6 & inflated \\\\\n278\tProbe BGE & 0.9413 & 0.85 & $-$9.1 & inflated \\\\\n279\tLLM-judge & 0.6625 & 0.68 & $+$1.8 & deflated \\\\\n280\t\\bottomrule\n281\t\\end{tabular}\n282\t\\caption{Per-method circularity gap. The 8.5pp structural gap decomposes the in-domain 0.9951 into 0.91 genuine signal + 0.085 circular artifact.}\n283\t\\label{tab:circularity}\n284\t\\end{table}\n285\t\n286\tThe in-domain 0.9951 decomposes into 0.91 genuine signal and 8.5pp circular artifact.\n287\tThe circularity artifact accounts for $\\sim$8.5\\% of the LF-derived score---significant but not fatal. The structural method's genuine signal of 0.91 remains 9pp above the best probe and 23pp above LLM-as-judge. The hierarchy is invariant to evaluation protocol: whether using LF-derived or gold labels, structural features are the best choice. This decomposition framework generalizes to any weak supervision pipeline where features overlap with labeling function inputs.\n288\t\n289\t\n290\t\\subsection{Gold-Validated Temporal Onset Alignment}\n291\t\\label{sec:temporal}\n292\t\n293\tTo validate Claim~2, we identify 88 stagnation onset boundaries in the gold set (transitions from progress to stagnation) and measure alignment with LF firing onsets using $\\pm$2 step tolerance (Table~\\ref{tab:temporal-gold}).\n294\t\n295\t\\begin{table}[t]\n296\t\\centering\n297\t\\small\n298\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n299\t\\toprule\n300\tLF & LF-Der Lag & Gold Lag & Align\\% & Tolerance \\\\\n301\t\\midrule\n302\tcmd\\_repetition & $-$1.87 & $-$1.5 & 85\\% & $\\pm$2 steps \\\\\n303\tfile\\_diff\\_delta & $-$1.2 & $-$0.8 & 78\\% & $\\pm$2 steps \\\\\n304\toutcome\\_backprop & $+$6.7 & $+$7.4 & 73\\% & $\\pm$2 steps \\\\\n305\t\\midrule\n306\tOverall & & & 82\\% & \\\\\n307\t\\bottomrule\n308\t\\end{tabular}\n309\t\\caption{Gold-validated temporal onset alignment. LF temporal roles (leading/lagging) are confirmed on human-annotated boundaries ($N{=}88$ onset boundaries).}\n310\t\\label{tab:temporal-gold}\n311\t\\end{table}\n312\t\n313\tOverall alignment is 82\\%, with all three LF temporal roles confirmed: command repetition leads stagnation onset ($-$1.5 steps, 85\\% aligned), file diff is concurrent ($-$0.8 steps, 78\\%), and outcome backpropagation lags ($+$7.4 steps, 73\\%).\n314\tGold-derived lag values closely match LF-derived values (differences $<$1 step), confirming that LF temporal dynamics reflect genuine agent behavior rather than labeling artifacts.\n315\t\n316\t\n317\t\\subsection{Cross-Agent Structural Validation}\n318\t\\label{sec:cross-agent}\n319\t\n320\tWe assess cross-agent generalization by estimating structural performance on OpenHands (OH) via bounded circularity transfer (Table~\\ref{tab:cross-agent}).\n321\t\n322\t\\begin{table}[t]\n323\t\\centering\n324\t\\small\n325\t\\begin{tabular}{lcc}\n326\t\\toprule\n327\tSetting & Macro-\\fone{} & Evidence Type \\\\\n328\t\\midrule\n329\tSWE Structural (LF) & 0.9951 & Circular eval \\\\\n330\tSWE Structural (Gold) & 0.91 & Direct gold \\\\\n331\tSWE Circularity gap & 8.5pp & Measured \\\\\n332\t\\midrule\n333\tOH Structural (LF) & 0.9674 & Circular eval \\\\\n334\tOH Structural (Gold est.) & 0.87--0.92 & Bounded estimate \\\\\n335\tOH Probe FT (LF) & 0.751 & Non-circular ref \\\\\n336\t\\bottomrule\n337\t\\end{tabular}\n338\t\\caption{Cross-agent structural performance. Even the conservative gold estimate (0.87) exceeds the OH probe by 12pp.}\n339\t\\label{tab:cross-agent}\n340\t\\end{table}\n341\t\n342\tOn SWE-Agent, the measured circularity gap is 8.5pp. Applying this range to OH's LF-derived 0.9674 yields a bounded gold estimate of 0.87--0.92. Even at the conservative lower bound (0.87), structural features outperform the OH fine-tuned probe (0.751) by 12pp, confirming that structural stagnation signals transfer across agent architectures because they capture universal behavioral patterns (command repetition, absence of code changes) rather than agent-specific semantics.\n343\t\n344\t\\subsection{\\fstagone{} Per-Class Analysis}\n345\t\\label{sec:fstag-analysis}\n346\t\n347\tStagnation is the minority class and the practically important one. We analyze \\fstagone{} along three dimensions.\n348\t\n349\t\\paragraph{By LF Confidence.}\n350\tTable~\\ref{tab:fstag-confidence} shows that probe stagnation detection is strongly tied to label quality: \\fstagone{} is 0.863 at $\\text{conf}{=}2$ but estimated at $\\sim$0.63 for $\\text{conf}{=}1$. This is a label limitation, not a probe failure---gold validation shows $\\text{conf}{=}1$ LF accuracy is only 0.69.\n351\t\n352\t\\begin{table}[t]\n353\t\\centering\n354\t\\small\n355\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccccc}\n356\t\\toprule\n357\tConf & $N$ & Stag $N$ & \\fstagone{} & Prec & Recall & Note \\\\\n358\t\\midrule\n359\tconf=2 & 1780 & 473 & 0.863 & 0.85 & 0.88 & Main set \\\\\n360\tconf=3 & 44 & 12 & 0.727 & 0.67 & 0.83 & Small $N$ \\\\\n361\tconf=1$^*$ & --- & --- & $\\sim$0.63 & $\\sim$0.48 & $\\sim$0.88 & Estimated \\\\\n362\t\\bottomrule\n363\t\\end{tabular}\n364\t\\caption{\\fstagone{} by LF confidence on the test set ($N{=}1824$). $^*$Estimated from confidence-gradient disagreement analysis.}\n365\t\\label{tab:fstag-confidence}\n366\t\\end{table}\n367\t\n368\t\\paragraph{By Trajectory Position.}\n369\tTable~\\ref{tab:fstag-position} reveals a U-shaped \\fstagone{} curve: early 0.83, middle 0.80, late 0.88. The middle-trajectory dip reflects transition zones where agents may be temporarily slowing rather than truly stagnating, leading to elevated false positive rates (10.2\\% vs.\\ 6.5\\% at endpoints).\n370\t\n371\t\\begin{table}[t]\n372\t\\centering\n373\t\\small\n374\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccccc}\n375\t\\toprule\n376\tPosition & $N$ & Stag $N$ & \\fstagone{} & Prec & Recall & Err\\% \\\\\n377\t\\midrule\n378\tEarly 0--33\\% & 351 & 64 & 0.83 & 0.80 & 0.86 & 6.6 \\\\\n379\tMid 33--66\\% & 353 & 108 & 0.80 & 0.75 & 0.86 & 10.2 \\\\\n380\tLate 66--100\\% & 1070 & 313 & 0.88 & 0.86 & 0.91 & 6.5 \\\\\n381\t\\bottomrule\n382\t\\end{tabular}\n383\t\\caption{\\fstagone{} by trajectory position. Middle-trajectory stagnation is hardest to detect due to ambiguous transition zones.}\n384\t\\label{tab:fstag-position}\n385\t\\end{table}\n386\t\n387\t\\paragraph{By Stagnation Subtype.}\n388\tTable~\\ref{tab:fstag-subtype} decomposes detection by behavioral pattern. Repetitive-command stagnation is easiest to detect (\\fstagone{} 0.91), no-file-change stagnation is intermediate (0.84), and outcome-only stagnation---where actions are behaviorally diverse but ultimately ineffective---is hardest (0.73). This gradient directly reflects the structural nature of stagnation signals: the first two subtypes have explicit structural markers (command repetition, file diff absence), while outcome-only stagnation requires deeper understanding unavailable to either structural features or embedding probes.\n389\t\n390\t\\begin{table}[t]\n391\t\\centering\n392\t\\small\n393\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n394\t\\toprule\n395\tSubtype & $N$ & \\fstagone{} & Prec & Recall \\\\\n396\t\\midrule\n397\tRepetitive commands & 320 & 0.91 & 0.89 & 0.93 \\\\\n398\tNo file changes & 95 & 0.84 & 0.81 & 0.87 \\\\\n399\tOutcome-only & 70 & 0.73 & 0.69 & 0.78 \\\\\n400\t\\midrule\n401\tOverall & 485 & 0.86 & 0.84 & 0.89 \\\\\n402\t\\bottomrule\n403\t\\end{tabular}\n404\t\\caption{\\fstagone{} by stagnation subtype. Outcome-only stagnation (behaviorally diverse but ineffective) is the hardest case for all methods.}\n405\t\\label{tab:fstag-subtype}\n406\t\\end{table}\n407\t\n408\t\n409\t\\subsection{Error Decomposition: Probe vs.\\ Structural}\n410\t\\label{sec:error-decomp}\n411\t\n412\tTable~\\ref{tab:error-decomp} compares the error distributions of the probe and structural classifier on LF-derived labels, revealing a strict subset relationship.\n413\t\n414\t\\begin{table}[t]\n415\t\\centering\n416\t\\small\n417\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n418\t\\toprule\n419\tMetric & Probe & Struct GB & Note \\\\\n420\t\\midrule\n421\t\\fstagone{} & 0.859 & 0.993 & $-$13.4pp gap \\\\\n422\tTotal errors & 131 & 7 & \\\\\n423\tFP (pred stag wrong) & 85 & 3 & \\\\\n424\tFN (miss stag) & 46 & 4 & \\\\\n425\tProbe-only errors & 124 & --- & Struct correct \\\\\n426\tShared errors & 7 & 7 & LF boundary \\\\\n427\tHigh-conf errors & 73\\% & 0\\% & \\\\\n428\t\\bottomrule\n429\t\\end{tabular}\n430\t\\caption{Error decomposition on LF-derived labels. All 7 structural errors are shared with the probe; 124 probe errors occur where the structural model is correct.}\n431\t\\label{tab:error-decomp}\n432\t\\end{table}\n433\t\n434\tThe structural model's 7 errors are a strict subset of the probe's 131 errors (5.3\\% overlap). All structural errors are borderline LF cases; 73\\% of probe errors are high-confidence false positives---systematic blind spots rather than borderline confusion. On gold labels, the gap narrows (estimated 0.87 vs.\\ 0.75 \\fstagone{}, $\\sim$12pp) as both methods struggle with outcome-only stagnation, but the structural advantage persists.\n435\t\n436\t\n437\t%======================================================================\n438\t\\section{Discussion}\n439\t\\label{sec:discussion}\n440\t%======================================================================\n441\t\n442\t\\paragraph{Why Are Structural Features Sufficient?}\n443\tStagnation in coding agents manifests through observable behavioral patterns: repeating the same commands, failing to produce code changes, and cycling through ineffective strategies.\n444\tThese patterns are directly captured by structural features (command repetition ratio, file diff count) without requiring semantic understanding of the actions.\n445\tThe gold-validated feature importance stability ($\\rho{=}0.96$) confirms that the same features matter whether evaluated against LF or human labels, and the structural advantage over probes (6--9pp on gold) persists across three different embedding encoders (Qwen3, E5, BGE), ruling out encoder quality as a confound.\n446\t\n447\t\\paragraph{Circularity: Honest Accounting.}\n448\tThe LF-derived evaluation inflates structural scores by $\\sim$8.5pp because structural features are a superset of LF inputs.\n449\tHowever, this inflation is symmetric: probes suffer comparable circularity gaps (8.6--9.6pp), meaning the relative ordering is unaffected.\n450\tThe gold evaluation provides the definitive answer: structural features genuinely detect stagnation better than learned representations, and this advantage is practically significant (6--9pp in macro-\\fone{}).\n451\t\n452\t\\paragraph{LLM-as-Judge: Orthogonal but Weak.}\n453\tThe LLM-judge is the only method whose gold performance exceeds its LF-derived score (+1.8pp), confirming orthogonal error patterns.\n454\tHowever, its absolute performance (0.68) is 23pp below structural features, with high cost (\\$0.006/window) and latency ($\\sim$800ms).\n455\tThe LLM's primary failure mode is low stagnation recall (0.52): it tends to give agents the benefit of the doubt, judging stagnation as progress.\n456\t\n457\t\\paragraph{Temporal Label Models: Why They Fail.}\n458\tDespite well-characterized i.i.d.\\ violations in the LF outputs (systematic leading/lagging temporal roles), three temporal label model implementations fail to improve over majority vote.\n459\tThe root cause is the low-conflict regime: with $\\text{conf}{\\geq}2$ achieving 95.1\\% internal consistency and 93\\% gold accuracy, there is little room for temporal alignment to improve labels.\n460\tTime-weighted voting primarily modifies low-confidence labels ($\\text{conf}{=}1$), but modifies them in the wrong direction, degrading downstream classifiers by up to 21.7pp on cross-agent transfer.\n461\t\n462\t\\paragraph{Limitations.}\n463\tOur study covers two agent frameworks (SWE-Agent and OpenHands) in the software engineering domain. Generalization to non-SE agents (web browsing, scientific research) requires additional validation. The gold set ($N{=}650$) is drawn from SWE-Agent only; cross-agent gold annotation is future work (bounded estimates suggest 0.87--0.92 for OH). The window size $k{=}5$ was selected empirically; optimal granularity may vary by agent and task type.\n464\t\n465\t\n466\t%======================================================================\n467\t\\section{Conclusion}\n468\t\\label{sec:conclusion}\n469\t%======================================================================\n470\t\n471\tWe present a systematic empirical study of stagnation detection in LLM agents via weak supervision.\n472\tOur central finding is that stagnation signals are fundamentally structural: even on human-annotated ground truth, 30-dimensional structural features outperform neural probes by 6--9pp and LLM-as-judge by 23pp, confirming that agent stagnation manifests through observable behavioral patterns rather than subtle semantic cues.\n473\tHuman annotation of 650 windows precisely quantifies the circularity artifact at 8.5pp while validating the structural$\\,{>}\\,$probe$\\,{>}\\,$LLM hierarchy.\n474\tOur temporal analysis reveals systematic but agent-specific LF dynamics, and our well-motivated negative result on temporal label models constrains future research in sequential weak supervision.\n475\tThese findings redirect agent monitoring from expensive representation learning toward lightweight, interpretable structural approaches.\n476\t\n477\t\n478\t\\begin{thebibliography}{30}\n479\t\n480\t\\bibitem[Yang et~al.(2024)]{yang2024sweagent}\n481\tJohn Yang, Carlos~E. Jimenez, Alexander Wettig, Kilian Lieret, Shunyu Yao, Karthik Narasimhan, and Ofir Press. 2024.\n482\t\\newblock {SWE}-agent: Agent-computer interfaces enable automated software engineering.\n483\t\\newblock In \\emph{NeurIPS}.\n484\t\n485\t\\bibitem[Wang et~al.(2024)]{wang2024openhands}\n486\tXingyao Wang, Boxuan Li, Yufan Song, Frank~F. Xu, Xiangru Tang, Mingchen Zhuge, Jiayi Pan, Yueqi Song, Bowen Li, Jaskirat Singh, Hoang~H. Tran, Fuqiang Li, Ren Ma, Mingzhang Zheng, Bill Qian, Yanjun Shao, Niklas Muennighoff, Ziyi Zhang, Botao Yu, Mengze Yu, Zijian Hu, Chuanbo Zheng, and Graham Neubig. 2024.\n487\t\\newblock {OpenHands}: An open platform for {AI} software developers as generalist agents.\n488\t\\newblock \\emph{arXiv:2407.16741}.\n489\t\n490\t\\bibitem[Jiang et~al.(2024)]{jiang2024cdcs}\n491\tShuyang Jiang, Yuhao Wang, and Yu Wang. 2024.\n492\t\\newblock Detecting cycles in agent trajectories.\n493\t\\newblock \\emph{arXiv preprint}.\n494\t\n495\t\\bibitem[Xi et~al.(2026)]{xi2026agentprm}\n496\tZhiheng Xi, Yiwen Ding, Wenxiang Chen, Boyang Hong, Honglin Guo, Junzhe Wang, Dingwen Yang, Chenyang Liao, Xin Guo, Wei He, Songyang Gao, Lu Chen, Rui Zheng, Yicheng Zou, Tao Gui, Qi Zhang, Xipeng Qiu, Xuanjing Huang, Zuxuan Wu, and Yu-Gang Jiang. 2026.\n497\t\\newblock {AgentPRM}: Process reward model as policy for agent learning.\n498\t\\newblock In \\emph{ACM Web Conference}.\n499\t\n500\t\\bibitem[Shinn et~al.(2023)]{shinn2023reflexion}\n501\tNoah Shinn, Federico Cassano, Ashwin Gopinath, Karthik Narasimhan, and Shunyu Yao. 2023.\n502\t\\newblock Reflexion: Language agents with verbal reinforcement learning.\n503\t\\newblock In \\emph{NeurIPS}.\n504\t\n505\t\\bibitem[Zhou et~al.(2023)]{zhou2023lats}\n506\tAndy Zhou, Kai Yan, Michal Shlapentokh-Rothman, Haohan Wang, and Yu-Xiong Wang. 2023.\n507\t\\newblock Language agent tree search unifies reasoning acting and planning in language models.\n508\t\\newblock In \\emph{ICML}.\n509\t\n510\t\\bibitem[Yao et~al.(2023)]{yao2023tree}\n511\tShunyu Yao, Dian Yu, Jeffrey Zhao, Izhak Shafran, Thomas~L. Griffiths, Yuan Cao, and Karthik Narasimhan. 2023.\n512\t\\newblock Tree of thoughts: Deliberate problem solving with large language models.\n513\t\\newblock In \\emph{NeurIPS}.\n514\t\n515\t\\bibitem[Ratner et~al.(2017)]{ratner2017snorkel}\n516\tAlexander Ratner, Stephen~H. Bach, Henry Ehrenberg, Jason Fries, Sen Wu, and Christopher R{\\'e}. 2017.\n517\t\\newblock Snorkel: Rapid training data creation with weak supervision.\n518\t\\newblock In \\emph{VLDB}.\n519\t\n520\t\\bibitem[Safranchik et~al.(2020)]{safranchik2020weakly}\n521\tEsteban Safranchik, Shiying Luo, and Stephen~H. Bach. 2020.\n522\t\\newblock Weakly supervised sequence tagging from noisy rules.\n523\t\\newblock In \\emph{AAAI}.\n524\t\n525\t\\bibitem[Shin et~al.(2022)]{shin2022universalizing}\n526\tGihyuk Shin, Vamsi~K. Ithapu, and Siddharth Bhatia. 2022.\n527\t\\newblock Universalizing weak supervision.\n528\t\\newblock In \\emph{ICLR}.\n529\t\n530\t\\bibitem[{Agent Monitor}(2025)]{agentmonitor2025iclr}\n531\t{Agent Monitor}. 2025.\n532\t\\newblock Monitoring {LLM} agents for sequentially contextual harm.\n533\t\\newblock In \\emph{ICLR Workshop}.\n534\t\n535\t\\bibitem[{Weak-to-Strong Monitoring}(2025)]{weakstrong2025}\n536\t{Weak-to-Strong Monitoring}. 2025.\n537\t\\newblock Reliable weak-to-strong monitoring.\n538\t\\newblock \\emph{arXiv:2508.19461}.\n539\t\n540\t\\bibitem[{SlopCodeBench}(2026)]{slopcodebench2026}\n541\t{SlopCodeBench}. 2026.\n542\t\\newblock {SlopCodeBench}: Quantifying coding agent degradation in long-horizon tasks.\n543\t\\newblock \\emph{arXiv:2603.24755}.\n544\t\n545\t\\bibitem[{SpecRA}(2025)]{specra2025}\n546\t{SpecRA}. 2025.\n547\t\\newblock Speculative repetition detection in agent traces.\n548\t\\newblock \\emph{arXiv preprint}.\n549\t\n550\t\\bibitem[{TrajAD}(2025)]{trajad2025}\n551\t{TrajAD}. 2025.\n552\t\\newblock Trajectory anomaly detection for {LLM} agents.\n553\t\\newblock \\emph{arXiv preprint}.\n554\t\n555\t\\bibitem[{PRM Framework}(2025)]{prm2025framework}\n556\t{PRM Framework}. 2025.\n557\t\\newblock A framework for process reward models.\n558\t\\newblock \\emph{arXiv:2502.10325}.\n559\t\n560\t\\bibitem[{ToolPRMBench}(2026)]{toolprmbench2026}\n561\t{ToolPRMBench}. 2026.\n562\t\\newblock {ToolPRMBench}: Benchmarking process reward models for tool use.\n563\t\\newblock \\emph{arXiv:2601.12294}.\n564\t\n565\t\\bibitem[{Trajectory Reward}(2026)]{trajreward2026}\n566\t{Trajectory Reward}. 2026.\n567\t\\newblock Trajectory-level reward modeling for {LLM} agents.\n568\t\\newblock \\emph{arXiv:2604.08178}.\n569\t\n570\t\\end{thebibliography}\n571\t\n572\t\\end{document}\n573\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_012ZnjAgqYU8U6zVNy6ESCYV","session_id":"4375801c-8206-4a72-bdc9-833c067fbe9b","uuid":"261c1012-ce5b-42e4-a142-341d16363449","timestamp":"2026-05-14T22:16:29.929Z"}
